            "region": "NL",
            "shares": 362,
            "caption": "Chris Voss spent his career negotiating with people who had hostages, then wrote Never Split the Difference. His techniques are specific enough that Claude can actually run them: say their objection before they do, name the emotion in the room, ask questions that make them solve your problem. 1| The accusation audit Here's the situation: [paste]. List every negative thing they could already be thinking about me, my position or my ask. Be blunt, include the ones I'd rather not hear. Then write my opening line so I say the worst of them out loud before they can. 2| The label pass Read their last message again. Name what they're actually feeling and what they're worried about underneath it. Write three labels I could use, each starting with \"it sounds like\", \"it looks like\" or \"it seems like\". No \"I\" statements, nothing that sounds like I'm diagnosing them. 3| The calibrated questions Write five open questions starting with how or what that make them explain their own constraints to me. Nothing answerable with yes or no. Include one version of \"how am I supposed to do that\" that doesn't sound aggressive in writing. 4| The that's right test Summarize their position back to them so completely that their only honest reply is \"that's right\". Not \"you're right\", which means they want me to stop talking. Then tell me what I still don't know about what they want. 5| The pressure test Play them. Push back on my position as hard as they realistically would. Find the exact point where I'd cave, tell me what I'd give away, and what I should have prepared instead. Run 5 before the conversation, not after. Finding your own weak point in private is the entire advantage. Save this, then run number 1 on the hardest thread sitting in your inbox right now. #claude #claudeai #negotiation #chrisvoss #neversplitthedifference   ",
